A||||6||Spektrum-87GT||ok... how hard is the belt job on a fiero?||||1||
Z||000000||Spektrum-87GT||11-16-2001||06:28 PM||||haynes doesnt cover it at all, and its just plain wierd... no visible tensioner(that i could find)<P>I've got a date tomorrow night and am stuck with a decision... attempt to change the belt and risk disabling my vehicle if i dont get it done<P>or <P>having to live with the embarrasment of a 'WIRRRRRRR' that comes and goes<P>help!<P>------------------<BR>restoration/modification on hold until who knows when<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/spektrum_87gt/" TARGET=_blank>87 Fiero GT</A><BR>1991 Jeep Cherokee<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://members.home.com/bspracklin1/sig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||avengador1||11-16-2001||06:41 PM||||If it's a V-6 it can be done, but it is very tight. There is no tensioner and it would probably help to have someone with a prybar apply pressure to the alternator while you tighten the bolts. I assume that is the belt you want to replace. All you have to do is loosen the upper and lower bolts and slacken the old belt to remove it. The lower bolt is a pita to get to but it can be done. Reverse the process to install the new belt with assistance mentioned above. You should be able to do it in less than an hour if you can get to the bolts. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||11-16-2001||07:17 PM||||I get to the belt tension bolt on the alternator by jacking the right rear up, prying with a prybar with one hand, pulling the ratchet with other. Only takes a few minutes. If you have ac, its a seperate belt.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Spektrum-87GT||11-16-2001||07:55 PM||||ah, forget it... sounds like something i could mess up pretty bad. it goes away after it warms up anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000004||K2m4||11-17-2001||07:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Spektrum-87GT:<BR><B>ah, forget it... sounds like something i could mess up pretty bad. it goes away after it warms up anyway.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Dud its real easy, HAve faith in yourself! My GT i change by my self and it only takes me 15 mins. I dont jak the car up, i just wedge my self under the pasenger side rear of the vehicle and the alternator is right there. Loosrn that tensioner bolt and pivot the alternator so you can take the old belt off from above. Put the new belt on this is where it gets tricky. Get someone from up top to use a broom stick and use it as a tensioner to make that belt tight egough and torque the bolt down...Voila!!! and your done...when it schreehes again loosen the bolt up and re tension the pig again. it doesnt take long at all and the only tools you need is a 13 mm wrench and a broomstick<P>Kerm<BR>86 2M4<BR>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||11-17-2001||08:10 PM||||Yeah, its real easy, I have to tighten mine every now and then, like tonight, and only took about 10 minutes to tighten up.||||reg||1
Z||000006||My7Fieros||11-17-2001||09:07 PM||||Well you're prob on your date as I speak ( <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<BR>but for a temp fix, use the goop brand belt dressing..itll fix that squeek temp (Ive never had any luck w/ the spray stuff).<BR>Also, when you change your belt, be sure to use a Dayco brand belt. Kelly/Springfield and other brands tend to squeek constantly.||||reg||1
